[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m returns 0 on both paths, and every `printf` return value is discarded.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found and fixed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Exit status and stderr on a failing stdout (ENOSPC)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproducer: `echo 42 | ./driver > /dev/full`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | stdout | stderr | status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | (nothing written) | empty | `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before) | (nothing written) | `thread 'main' panicked ... failed printing to stdout: No space left on device (os error 28)` | `134` (SIGABRT) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Cause: the translation used the `print!` macro. `print!` **panics** when the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m underlying write fails. The C code calls `printf` and throws the return value
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m away, so a failed write is invisible and `main` still returns 0. With
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `panic = "abort"` in the release profile the panic became SIGABRT, so both the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit status and stderr diverged.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fix: `src/main.rs` now routes all output through a small `COut` struct that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m accumulates bytes in a `Vec<u8>` and performs a single `write_all` + `flush` at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit with both `Result`s discarded (`let _ = ...`). Buffering everything until
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit also matches glibc's behaviour here: `stdout` is fully buffered when it is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not a terminal and this program emits at most 8 short lines, well under
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `BUFSIZ`, so the real C program never flushes before `exit` either.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `stdout_write_error_is_ignored_like_c`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. SIGPIPE disposition — writing to a closed pipe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproducer: `echo 42 | ./driver | true` (reader exits before the writer writes)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | | status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | killed by signal 13 (shell reports `141`) |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before) | `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Cause: the Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN` before `main` runs. A C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program inherits the default disposition, so its write to the closed pipe kills
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it. The Rust binary instead got `EPIPE`, and once fix #1 made write errors
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m silent it exited 0 — a silent divergence in exit status with identical (empty)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Fix: `reset_sigpipe()` at the top of `main` restores `SIG_DFL` for `SIGPIPE`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m via a direct `extern "C" { fn signal(...) }` declaration (no new dependency).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `sigpipe_disposition_matches_c`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note that fixes #1 and #2 are independent: reverting either one alone makes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exactly one of the two tests fail. Both were verified that way.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours deliberately replicated, not "fixed"
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These looked like bugs in the C but are reproduced exactly.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Trailing garbage is accepted.** `parse_val` ignores `*endp`, so `"42abc"`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"2.5"`, `"1e5"`, `"1 2"`, `"0x10"` and `"1_000"` all parse as `42`, `2`, `1`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `1`, `0` and `1` respectively and take the success path. The Rust must not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   require the whole string to be consumed. (`accepts_trailing_garbage_after_digits`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Signed `int` overflow wraps.** `add_bedrooms` does `bedrooms += extra` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `run` is called twice, so `x = 2147483647` computes `5 + x` and then `5 + 2x`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   both of which overflow. This is UB in C, but the built binary wraps two's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   complement. The Rust uses `wrapping_add`; `saturating_add` or a plain `+`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (which panics in debug) would both diverge. (`bedrooms_overflow_wraps_like_c`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`fgets`, not `scanf`.** Only the first line is consumed and at most 99
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bytes. `"42\n99\n"` uses `42`. A 100-digit line is truncated to 99 digits,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   which then overflows and takes the *error* path; truncating a long line can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   also change the parsed value (`"0"*98 + "5" + "9"*20` parses as `5`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`fgets_reads_only_the_first_line`, `fgets_truncates_at_99_bytes`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Embedded NUL ends the string.** The buffer is NUL-terminated, so `"4\0002"`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   parses as `4`, and a leading NUL yields the empty string and the error path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`embedded_nul_terminates_the_c_string`, `rejects_leading_nul`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`errno == 0` vs. the range check are different rejections.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"9223372036854775807"` is rejected by the `INT_MAX` comparison with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `errno == 0`; `"9223372036854775808"` is rejected because `strtol` set
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `ERANGE`. Both print the same message, but the Rust `strtol` emulation has to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   model the `ERANGE` flag and the saturating return value separately from the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   range test. `strtol` must saturate to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"-9223372036854775808"` must *not* set `ERANGE`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`rejects_out_of_int_range_but_in_long_range`, `rejects_erange_overflow`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`strtol` whitespace set.** Space, `\t`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r`, `\n` are all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   skipped before the optional sign; a space *between* the sign and the digits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`"- 5"`) is not. (`accepts_leading_whitespace_forms`, `rejects_non_numeric`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`exit 0` on every input path**, including a failed `fgets` (closed stdin,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stdin is a directory). (`closed_stdin_matches_c`, `stdin_from_directory_matches_c`)
